From 3ef0cdc91d3a5f005dc42fc4271dab12455c89ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 17:57:11 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09083/11362] Correct two wrong measurements, and fix the
 checkpoint commit that ate them twice

Operator asked whether to re-run experiments to backfill the data the new
convention machinery reported missing. Measuring first gave the answer: the data
is largely not missing. Both numbers committed yesterday were wrong, and wrong in
this project's signature way.

  rows    claimed 3 of 60 artifacts carry per-unit rows. TRUE: 39 of 60. The
          check read only top-level keys; most rows sit nested one or two levels
          down.
  reasons claimed 37 of 58 blocked artifacts record no reason. TRUE: 6 of 54.
          `_REASON_FIELDS` omitted `gate_check_summary` -- the field the
          conductor's OWN pre-gate writes, and the one most of them use.

Two detectors, both blind, both reporting a corpus far worse than it is: a pattern
list narrower than the concept it names, inside the tools built to catch that
failure. Nothing was re-run on the strength of those numbers, which is the only
reason this is a correction and not an incident. So: no backfill. Re-running to
fill a gap that measurement invented would have burned days of GPU and rewritten
the record.

THE CORRECTED READING IS MORE USEFUL THAN THE WRONG ONE. Of 28 recurring blocks,
15 say the upstream readiness score was 0 when 1 was expected -- a gate doing its
job. The other 13 are a BROKEN CONTRACT between planner and agent: 9 say the
upstream artifact was never found, and 4 say the gated field read None with
unmistakable near-misses -- gated on `scorer_ready` where the artifact wrote
`ebcn_scorer_ready`, on `pwa_ready` where it wrote `pwa_kan_ready`, on
`ledger_ready` where it wrote `cerce_ledger_ready`. The planner names a gate
field, the agent writes a different one, nothing reconciles them, and a task with
no reason to be blocked is blocked. Planner guidance now says to spell the gate
field identically in the upstream task's own REQUIRED ARTIFACT FIELDS.

The ledger now prints WHY, not just the verdict. Grouping 28 blocks under
`blocked_gate_check_failed` said nothing actionable; each group now shows its two
most common reason shapes.

THE CHECKPOINT COMMIT, found because it destroyed this work mid-session, twice.
The conductor's "preserve uncommitted work from interrupted run" path ran
`git commit` WITHOUT --no-verify. pre-commit stashes unstaged changes, runs hooks,
and restores by `git apply` if one fails; when that patch does not apply, the work
is gone. This commit exists only to preserve work, so running a cycle that can
destroy it is backwards. It fired live today: the test-suite-mutation gate refused
(correctly -- a marker was armed by the conductor's own pytest run), the commit
aborted, and the edits had to be retyped from conversation memory.

The sibling path `git_commit_and_push()` was fixed for exactly this in 2026-05-03
and carries a long docstring explaining why. This path was missed and kept the
defect for three months, about 3400 lines below the explanation. Verification
still runs where it belongs: operator commits, agent-spawned commits, and CI.

One more substring bug, found inside the fix: the reason-normaliser stripped
`\bexp[\w-]*` and ate the word "expected", rendering every gate line as
`actual=N == <task>=N` and hiding the comparison it exists to show. Same shape as
`diffusiongemma_met` matching inside "meta". Now requires a digit after `exp`.

Marker retired deliberately, per the guard's own instructions. The two `results/`
paths it named were byte-identical to HEAD (`git diff HEAD` empty), so there was
no rewrite to publish.

